NAME
       lvcreate - create a logical volume in a volume group

SYNOPSIS
       lvcreate -n NAME -L SIZE VG [PV...]
       lvcreate -n NAME -l EXTENTS VG [PV...]
       lvcreate -s -n NAME -L SIZE VG/ORIGIN

DESCRIPTION
       LVM builds storage in three layers: physical volumes (PVs, disks or
       partitions prepared with pvcreate) are pooled into a volume group (VG,
       made with vgcreate), and logical volumes (LVs) are carved out of a VG.
       An LV is used like a partition (mkfs, mount, swap), but it can be
       grown later and can span several disks.

       lvcreate makes a new LV and its device files:

           /dev/VG/NAME   and   /dev/mapper/VG-NAME

       Both names point to the same device; either may be used in /etc/fstab
       (or use the file system's UUID).

   Sizes and extents
       A VG is divided into physical extents (PEs), 4 MiB each unless the VG
       was made with vgcreate -s. Every LV is a whole number of extents, so a
       size is rounded up to the next multiple of the extent size, and
       lvcreate says so ("Rounding up size to full physical extent").

       -L takes a size with a unit: 500M, 2G, 1.5G (k, m, g, t; capitals
       mean the same). -l takes a number of extents, or a percentage:

       -l 100         100 extents (400 MiB with 4 MiB extents)
       -l 100%FREE    all the free space in the VG
       -l 50%VG       half the size of the VG

       When a task asks for an LV of N extents, use -l; when it asks for a
       size, use -L. vgdisplay shows "PE Size" and "Free PE / Size".

OPTIONS
       -n NAME, --name NAME
              The LV's name.

       -L SIZE, --size SIZE
              The size, with a unit.

       -l N, --extents N
              The size in extents, or a percentage of FREE, VG or PVS.

       -s, --snapshot
              Make a snapshot of the LV ORIGIN: a copy of how it looks now,
              which needs only room for the changes made afterwards.

       --type TYPE
              The kind of LV: linear (the default), striped, raid1...

       -y, --yes
              Answer yes, for example to wiping an old signature found where
              the LV is made.

       -W y|n, --wipesignatures y|n
              Wipe (or keep) old file system signatures in the new space.

       -Z y|n, --zero y|n
              Zero the start of the new LV.

EXAMPLES
       Make a 1 GiB LV and an xfs file system on it, mounted at boot:

           pvcreate /dev/vdb1
           vgcreate vgdata /dev/vdb1
           lvcreate -n lvdata -L 1G vgdata
           mkfs.xfs /dev/vgdata/lvdata
           mkdir /data
           echo "/dev/vgdata/lvdata /data xfs defaults 0 0" >> /etc/fstab
           mount -a

       A VG with 8 MiB extents and an LV of exactly 50 extents (400 MiB):

           vgcreate -s 8M vgapp /dev/vdc1
           lvcreate -n lvapp -l 50 vgapp

       Use all the free space left in the VG:

           lvcreate -n lvrest -l 100%FREE vgdata

       An LV for swap:

           lvcreate -n lvswap -L 512M vgdata
           mkswap /dev/vgdata/lvswap
           echo "/dev/vgdata/lvswap none swap defaults 0 0" >> /etc/fstab
           swapon -a

       Check the result:

           lvs vgdata

NOTES
       To make an LV bigger later, use lvextend -r (it grows the file system
       too); xfs can grow but never shrink.

SEE ALSO
       lvm(8), pvcreate(8), vgcreate(8), lvextend(8), lvs(8), lvdisplay(8),
       lvremove(8)
